    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "“Seek knowledge even unto China,” or in its original Arabic version, “أطلب العلم ولو في الصين,” is an ancient Arabic proverb that emphasizes the pursuit of knowledge, even if it requires traveling to distant lands as far as China. In the late 20th and early 21st centuries, China adopted a noninterventionist policy in the Middle East, as Beijing often calls it, but others believe China has begun a new phase of active engagement. In this new engagement, China has avoided overtly choosing sides in any of the ongoing tension points in the Middle East, a challenging feat in today’s world of great power competitions. Although Beijing has not been overtly involved in the Middle East in the same manner as the United States (e.g., fighting in wars), it has paid increasing attention to the region. After decades of exponential economic growth, China has shifted its policies from almost completely inward-looking and regional to embracing a global and multilateral approach. China’s amicable relationships with the Middle East are part of the Middle Kingdom’s broader vision of reviving the “China Dream” (中国梦) and strengthening its global influence. \r\n\r\nThis paper focuses on six Arab monarchies within the Persian Gulf: the UAE, Qatar, Bahrain, Kuwait, Saudi Arabia, and Oman, but Iran is also mentioned in the third part of this paper when I analyze China’s diplomatic breakthrough while working together with Saudi Arabia. China’s ambition to enhance its global soft power is closely linked to its objective of strengthening people-to-people cultural diplomacy (民心相通). Beijing uses three primary strategies to leverage its soft-power influence in the Middle East. The first involves establishing educational and cultural programs, including both public and private Chinese language schools. The second is through investing in renewable energy and infrastructure. The third strategy is using tactical public diplomacy. China actively seeks Middle Eastern and Persian Gulf allies while exercising its soft power to gain powerful international support on critical issues such as Taiwan, the Dalai Lama, and the Uyghur situation in Xinjiang.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
